1
00:00:00,949 --> 00:00:09,738
Welcome to Asia episode seven of seven. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,387 --> 00:00:29,719
Our final episode is longer, with eleven countries from mainland Southeast
Asia into the island world. Singapore uses a labelled supplemental map;
Malaysia and Indonesia need special attention because their territory spans
separated regions and islands.

3
00:00:30,399 --> 00:00:34,324
Let's bring Myanmar and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:42,511 --> 00:00:52,960
Myanmar lies east of Bangladesh and India, west of Thailand and Laos, and
south of China. Its long western and southern shores face the Bay of Bengal
and Andaman Sea.

5
00:00:53,689 --> 00:01:05,496
Bagan spreads temples and stupas across a plain beside the Ayeyarwady River.
The landscape records the religious and artistic achievements of an early
Buddhist kingdom.

6
00:01:06,153 --> 00:01:16,730
Picture temple silhouettes along a river plain at mainland Southeast Asia’s
western edge. Myanmar is west of Thailand, not on the eastern Vietnamese
coast.

7
00:01:20,026 --> 00:01:31,671
Thailand occupies the middle of the mainland group, between Myanmar and Laos,
above Cambodia and Malaysia. Its southern arm follows the peninsula toward
Malaysia.

8
00:01:32,324 --> 00:01:43,156
Sukhothai was an early capital of Siam. Its monuments show how several
cultural influences combined into a distinctive style of Thai art and
architecture.

9
00:01:43,821 --> 00:01:54,525
Remember Thailand as a broad northern body with a long southern handle. Place
old Sukhothai in the mainland portion, then follow the handle toward Malaysia.

10
00:01:55,283 --> 00:01:58,952
Let's bring Laos and its neighbours into view.

11
00:02:04,902 --> 00:02:16,036
Laos is landlocked, east of Thailand and west of Vietnam, with China to the
north and Cambodia to the south. It is the inland member of this mainland
chain.

12
00:02:16,693 --> 00:02:31,751
The Plain of Jars contains huge stone vessels linked to Iron Age funerary
practices. Archaeological evidence connects them with burials, even though
many details remain under study.

13
00:02:32,411 --> 00:02:42,001
Picture stone jars inland between Thailand and Vietnam. Rivers cross Laos, but
no part of the country reaches the sea.

14
00:02:44,558 --> 00:02:54,241
Cambodia sits below Laos, southeast of Thailand and west of southern Vietnam.
Its southwestern edge has a coast on the Gulf of Thailand.

15
00:02:54,895 --> 00:03:06,876
Angkor preserves the remains of successive Khmer capitals, including Angkor
Wat and the carved faces at the Bayon. The famous temple belongs to a much
larger historical landscape.

16
00:03:07,537 --> 00:03:17,684
Remember Angkor below Laos and inside Vietnam’s coastal curve. Cambodia has a
southwestern coast; Laos, immediately north, is landlocked.

17
00:03:20,290 --> 00:03:33,943
Vietnam follows the eastern coast of the mainland, below China and east of
Laos and Cambodia. Its long, narrow central stretch links broader northern and
southern regions.

18
00:03:34,597 --> 00:03:45,429
Ha Long Bay and the Cat Ba area contain limestone towers rising from the sea.
Water has flooded a karst landscape, leaving a remarkable scattering of rocky
islands.

19
00:03:46,085 --> 00:03:55,675
Picture limestone towers beside the long eastern coastal ribbon. Vietnam
curves around Laos and Cambodia; Thailand lies farther west.

20
00:03:56,409 --> 00:03:59,729
Let's bring Malaysia and its neighbours into view.

21
00:04:06,332 --> 00:04:19,683
Malaysia has two main regions: one on the Malay Peninsula below Thailand, the
other on northern Borneo. The South China Sea separates them; both belong to
the same country.

22
00:04:20,347 --> 00:04:32,967
Kinabalu Park, in Sabah on Borneo, rises from tropical rainforest into much
cooler mountain habitats. A single mountain landscape supports a remarkable
range of plant life.

23
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:47,273
Remember one country in two regions, with Mount Kinabalu on the Borneo side.
Singapore sits off the peninsula’s southern tip, while Brunei is tucked into
Borneo’s northern coast.

24
00:04:48,477 --> 00:04:54,061
Let's find Singapore on a labelled supplemental map, then move in for a closer
view.

25
00:05:00,362 --> 00:05:13,748
Singapore is the small island country immediately south of peninsular
Malaysia. Indonesia’s nearby islands lie across the strait; this labelled
supplemental map shows Singapore separately.

26
00:05:14,406 --> 00:05:26,771
Singapore Botanic Gardens became an important centre of tropical plant
research, including work connected with rubber cultivation. A garden helped
shape a major regional industry.

27
00:05:27,428 --> 00:05:38,771
Picture a research garden at the peninsula’s southern tip. Singapore is its
own country, not a city inside Malaysia, despite the very short crossing
between them.

28
00:05:39,623 --> 00:05:43,582
Let's bring Brunei and its neighbours into view.

29
00:05:46,639 --> 00:05:56,658
Brunei is a small country on Borneo’s northern coast, surrounded on land by
Malaysia. Malaysian territory separates its two main parts.

30
00:05:57,313 --> 00:06:08,656
Mangroves along the Brunei River are habitat for proboscis monkeys, famous for
their large noses. The wildlife hook belongs to Borneo, not the Malay
Peninsula.

31
00:06:09,313 --> 00:06:20,528
Picture the long-nosed monkey on Borneo’s north coast. Keep Brunei small and
coastal, with Malaysian territory around it and Indonesian Borneo farther
south.

32
00:06:21,312 --> 00:06:25,271
Let's bring Indonesia and its neighbours into view.

33
00:06:35,193 --> 00:06:49,775
Indonesia stretches across a vast chain of islands between the Indian and
Pacific Oceans. It includes Sumatra, Java, much of Borneo, Sulawesi and the
western part of New Guinea.

34
00:06:50,429 --> 00:07:04,593
Komodo dragons are giant lizards native to a small part of Indonesia. Their
island habitats are protected in Komodo National Park: real reptiles behind a
dragon’s name.

35
00:07:05,251 --> 00:07:19,880
Picture the dragon among a long east-west island chain. Borneo is shared with
Malaysia and Brunei, and New Guinea is shared with Papua New Guinea; neither
whole island is exclusively Indonesian.

36
00:07:20,658 --> 00:07:23,804
Let's bring Philippines and its neighbours into view.

37
00:07:33,506 --> 00:07:45,836
The Philippines is the island country east of Vietnam, north of Indonesia and
south of Taiwan. Luzon is toward the north, with Mindanao toward the south.

38
00:07:46,498 --> 00:08:00,012
In the Cordillera mountains, Ifugao rice terraces follow steep slopes in
carefully maintained steps. Farming knowledge and water management turned
mountainsides into productive fields.

39
00:08:00,665 --> 00:08:12,008
Remember a mostly north-south island group above Indonesia, with rice terraces
on northern Luzon. Keep it separate from Indonesia’s much longer east-west
chain.

40
00:08:12,701 --> 00:08:17,647
Let's bring Timor-Leste and its neighbours into view.

41
00:08:20,224 --> 00:08:32,024
Timor-Leste occupies the eastern part of Timor, plus the separate Oecusse
enclave and nearby islands. Indonesia occupies the western part of the main
island.

42
00:08:32,677 --> 00:08:44,531
Tais is a handwoven textile used in ceremonies, exchanges and community life.
Its colours and patterns carry identities and knowledge passed down by
Timorese weavers.

43
00:08:45,195 --> 00:08:58,454
Picture woven cloth at the eastern end of Timor, below Indonesia’s main island
chain. The small Oecusse piece also belongs to Timor-Leste, although
Indonesian territory surrounds it on land.

44
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:28,016
On the mainland, Myanmar is west, Thailand central, Laos inland, Cambodia
below Laos, and Vietnam along the eastern coast. Malaysia reaches down the
peninsula and across to Borneo; Singapore sits off the peninsula’s tip.

45
00:09:28,673 --> 00:09:40,956
Brunei is small and coastal on Borneo. Indonesia stretches east to west, the
Philippines forms a more north-south island group above it, and Timor-Leste
occupies eastern Timor with its separate enclave.

46
00:09:41,623 --> 00:10:01,747
Across seven episodes, we have visited forty-nine teaching stops. Cyprus
counts once, with its separate northern map target explained; Bahrain,
Maldives and Singapore use supplemental views. Recall each country through its
position and its memorable fact.
